==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|ang|þurh}} {{m|ang|ūt}}, equivalent to {{com|en|through|out}}. Compare {{cog|de|durchaus|t=all the way, fully, absolutely}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|thro͞o-out'}}, {{IPA|en|/θɹuːˈaʊt/}}
* {{a|Canada}} {{IPA|en|/θɹuˈʌʊt/}}
* {{a|reduced also}} {{IPA|en|/θɹaʊt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-throughout.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|aʊt|s=2}}

===Preposition===
{{en-prep}}

# In every part of; all through.
#: {{syn|en|across|amidst|during}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1748|author=David Hume|title=Enquiries concerning the human understanding and concerning the principles of moral.|location=London|publisher=Oxford University Press|year_published=1973|section=§5
|passage=And though a philosopher may live remote from business, the genius of philosophy, if carefully cultivated by several, must gradually diffuse itself '''throughout''' the whole society.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1913|author=[[w:Robert Barr (writer)|Robert Barr]]
|chapter=4|title=[http://openlibrary.org/works/OL1796924W Lord Stranleigh Abroad]
|passage=“My father had ideas about conservation long before the United States took it up.{{...}}You preserve water in times of flood and freshet to be used for power or for irrigation '''throughout''' the year.{{nb...}}”}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author=w:Edwin Black
|chapter=2|title=[http://openlibrary.org/works/OL4103950W Internal Combustion]
|passage=But through the oligopoly, charcoal fuel proliferated '''throughout''' London's trades and industries.&nbsp; By the 1200s, brewers and bakers, tilemakers, glassblowers, pottery producers, and a range of other craftsmen all became hour-to-hour consumers of charcoal.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Completely [[through]], right the way through.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1560| title=Arthur of Brytayn: the hystory of the moost noble and valyaunt knyght Arthur of lytell brytayne |author=John Bourchier, 2nd Baron Berners | page=| passage=His spere went clene '''thrughout''' hys body, and so he fell downe deed.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1561 | title=Seneca's Hercules Furens |author=John Heywood | page=| passage=The dedlye sworde '''throughout''' my brest to stryke I will applye.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1756| title=A New Edition of the Life and Heroick Actions of the renoun'd Sir William Wallace|author=William Hamilton| page=33| passage=His barnisht blad '''throughout''' his body share, }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1778| title=The History of English Poetry|author=Thomas Warton | page=| passage=Palamon at seeing Arcite , feels a ''colde fwerde'' glide '''throughout''' his heart: he starts from his ambuscade, and instantly salutes Areite with the appellation of ''false traitour''.}}

